New Week, New Opportunities. Welcome to Modern Investing Techniques, Episode 98 for July 6,

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:14.460
2026. I'm Patrick, coming to you from Vancouver. Monday means fresh setups. Let's break down the

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:20.200
week ahead and find our edge. Quick reminder, everything we discuss here is for education and

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.780
entertainment. The practice investment of the day uses simulated trades with no real money.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:32.200
I'm not a licensed financial advisor and this isn't financial advice. Always do your own research

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:39.060
before putting real money to work. Bank stock outperformance this quarter gives TFSA holders a

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:46.780
rotation signal into financials with volume confirmation required first. The S&amp;P 500 closed flat

00:00:46.780 --> 00:01:02.540
at 7483. The NASDAQ composite closed at 25,833 down 0.8%. The TSX composite rose 0.8% to 35,247.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:09.360
Tech futures rebounded while oil slipped after OPEC plus signaled an August output increase.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.100
Bank equities posted their strongest quarter since 2009 as financials led.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.800
The Bank of Canada next decision sits in late July with markets pricing steady rates,

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:28.500
while the Fed remains on hold through summer. Our simulated portfolio sits at plus 11.2% alpha

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:35.080
versus the NASDAQ across 35 benchmarked trades. That edge comes from waiting for confirmation

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:41.900
rather than chasing headlines. When oil prices exit the danger zone and roll over, history shows

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:47.980
equities, especially cyclical sectors, tend to accelerate because lower energy costs act

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:54.840
as a broad tax cut on consumers and businesses. The current swift pullback in crude creates exactly

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:01.820
that setup. Evercore ISI noted the move supplies a tailwind for the broader economy. Investors can

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:07.520
implement this by screening for sectors with depressed multiples that also show same day volume

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:15.180
above their 20-day average. Then rotate a small TFSA allocation into a broad financials or

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:22.500
industrials ETF. The pattern has worked best in post-recession recoveries when oil declines coincide

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:28.780
with stable or falling rates. The risk is a sudden geopolitical spike that reverses the oil drop

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:35.360
before equities price it in. Pair any rotation with the same day volume check to avoid false signals.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:42.200
This volume filter is the same discipline we teach in today's education segment. Imagine you bought

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:48.340
a bank ETF last week after the Globe and Mail noted the sector's best quarter since 2009

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:55.080
and the price ticked higher on light volume. Your order filled yet the next session the index reversed

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:01.340
because the move lacked participation from larger players. The mechanism is straightforward. Volume

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:06.980
below the 20-day average signals the advance rests on thin buying that can evaporate when broader

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:15.160
sentiment shifts. That turns a 2% gain into a 4% loss within two sessions. Professionals always check

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:21.820
whether the day's volume exceeds that average before treating any breakout as confirmed. Most retail

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:27.820
investors skip this step and chase the headline instead. The biggest mistake with momentum entries

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:34.280
is entering on price action alone. Instead always require volume above the 20-day average

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:40.700
on the same day the catalyst appears. That same volume discipline explains why we are not opening a

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:47.100
new position today. This is a simulated trade for educational purposes only. No new position

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:54.020
opened today. We are watching for any bank ETF to close above its 50-day moving average on volume

00:03:54.020 --> 00:04:01.340
exceeding the 20-day average. The catalyst is bank equities posting their strongest quarter since 2009

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:07.920
creating a potential rotation opportunity out of recent tech leadership. The risk assessment notes

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:14.580
the sector has lagged in prior growth rotations so any stop would sit 6% below entry to limit

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:22.240
drawdown. The target would be plus 4% to plus 8% over 5 trading days if volume confirms.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:28.680
Confidence level is low because this remains a single factor setup without volume or earnings

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:34.740
confirmation yet. The exercise demonstrates how to wait for multiple aligned factors before

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.860
committing capital instead of chasing headline strength. Listeners learn the discipline of naming

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:47.040
exact trigger levels rather than hoping for follow-through. No newly closed trade since the last

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.380
review. The most recent practice investment has already been reviewed. Current holdings remain open

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:59.340
and pending their scheduled evaluation so there is no new realized result to report today.

